\newlabel{fig:steepness-analysis}{{3}{18}{Effect of steepness parameter $\alpha $ on stability score. Left: $S_\alpha $ as a function of normalized variance $\hat {\sigma }$ for different $\alpha $ values. Right: Discrimination between consistent and inconsistent outputs peaks at $\alpha \approx 15$; we select $\alpha =20$ for balanced discrimination and score range}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:appendix-stability}{{4}{19}{Stability score analysis across LLMs on ShareGPT structured output tasks. (a) Box plots showing the distribution of $S_{\alpha }$ for all 18 evaluated models, ordered by median stability. The gradient coloring (red to green) reflects ranking. (b) Temperature degradation curves for five representative models: Claude-Opus-4.5 (most capable proprietary), Qwen3-235B-A22B (most capable open-source), GPT-4.1-Mini (cost-effective), Grok-4.1-Fast (fast inference), and Nova-2-Lite (AWS). Higher temperature generally reduces stability, though degradation patterns vary by model architecture}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{tab:metric-examples}{{22}{19}{Illustrative Examples: How Output Distributions Affect Metrics}{table.22}{}}
\newlabel{tab:real-metric-examples}{{23}{19}{Real Model Comparison: Metric Decomposition at Different Temperatures}{table.23}{}}
\newlabel{fig:pareto-frontier}{{5}{20}{Pareto frontier analysis for ShareGPT (left) and Toucan (right). The x-axis shows adjusted consistency ($C_{adj} = r_v \times C_{mean}$), combining validity and mean similarity. The y-axis shows stability score ($S_\alpha $). Models on the Pareto frontier (green dashed line) represent optimal trade-offs. \textbf {Key insight}: Claude-Opus-4.5 and Claude-Opus-4 dominate both frontiers, while task-specific gaps are evident---Llama-3.3-70B achieves moderate consistency on ShareGPT but falls to the lower-left on Toucan due to low validity (33\%)}{figure.5}{}}
